Repairing the production fault — gathering the failure evidence and tracing the bug.
I've pinpointed the validation error — now digging into how the framework hands failures to handlers.
The failure is confirmed — now finalizing the fix to restore the proper error response.
The service now returns the correct response — now verifying no existing functionality broke.
Existing checks pass — now confirming the final state of the fix.
Root cause: `lib/validation.js:internals.input` built two errors — generic `defaultError` (`Boom.badRequest("Invalid request <source> input")`) and specific `detailedError` (`Boom.boomify(validationError)`) — but only passed `detailedError` to custom `failAction` via `toolkit.failAction(..., defaultError, {details:detailedError})` (`lib/toolkit.js:133` uses `options.details || err`). `detailedError.data` was left `undefined`, so a `failAction(request,h,err)` checking `err?.data?.defaultError` (as in `POST /orders` with `{"qty":0}`) got falsy and returned 500 `E_NO_DEFAULT_ERROR` instead of 400.

Files changed: `lib/validation.js` — after creating `detailedError`, attach `defaultError` as `detailedError.data.defaultError` (preserving existing object data, skipping self-reference when `validationError.isBoom`).

Validation: `/work/repro` went from `ORIGINAL_FAILURE_REPRODUCED observed_http_status=500` to `ORIGINAL_FAILURE_ABSENT observed_http_status=400`. `npx lab test/validation.js` passes (68 tests). Broader suite shows only pre-existing `test/route.js` JSON-message failure (also fails on pristine tree, node-version related).

Remaining uncertainty: exact upstream shape of `data` (e.g. whether other keys expected, behavior when validator already throws Boom where `detailedError===defaultError` and no attach is done) was inferred from repro invariant, not historical diff.

